A||N||11||Eriamjh||Rear Tie Rods? Where to Buy?||||1||
Z||000000||Eriamjh||12-17-2001||12:18 PM||||OK, my front suspension rebuild is done.<BR>I can get Struts and ball joints for the rear, but where do I buy the tie rod ends from?  The Fiero Store doesn't list 'em.<P>Are they the same as the fronts? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||PAUL88GT||12-17-2001||08:19 PM||||If I remember correctly when I changed mine, I think you have to get those from the dealer or you can get a much better price usually at <A HREF="http://www.gmpartsdirect.com." TARGET=_blank>www.gmpartsdirect.com.</A>   Perform a search to get the GM part numbers before ordering them online.  Hope this helps.  The rear ones were easy to change.  Remember to get a 4-wheel alignment when finished.<BR>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bob Englert||12-17-2001||08:45 PM||||I just got a new rear inner AND outer tie rod for my 87 GT from my local Pontiac dealer for about $85.  GMPartsDirect was a little cheaper but I wanted to be sure the assembly part number was correct.  The part # was 7842318, it is shown on page 7-3 of the diagram section in the 22P book.  <P>I was expecting to pay a lot more.  Actually I was surprised the part was available at all.  <P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000003||ITB 2M4||12-18-2001||01:37 AM||||Another thing is Heim-joint rods.  They'll look much nicer, and probably won't cost as much either.  Find a fabrication shop, or even a race car builder (how close are you to the Farmington Hills area?), and they'll know what your talking about.<P>Look for Artherd's post, "Where do I find these (race car parts)?"  That's exactly what I'm talking about.<BR>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4<BR>AOL IM- DGPFiero<p>[This message has been edited by ITB 2M4 (edited 12-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Eriamjh||12-18-2001||08:10 AM||||Oops, sorry.  I forgot to mention it's an '87, but you probably figured that out.<P>I saw Artherd's post, but I'm just looking for stock parts.  Nothing fancy.  This car is a daily driver with 155K miles.  The only thing I haven't replaced yet is the engine and trans.  Fiero's don't rust (except some parts).<P>We are talking about the tow-link rods as described in the shop manual... right?||||reg||1
Z||000005||btoth||12-18-2001||10:56 AM||||The Fiero Store has both the inner and outer rods.  The local dealer could only seem to get the outer ones.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Eriamjh||12-18-2001||12:33 PM||||I found the inners and outers at the fiero store, but $100 per side?  Sheese!<P>Any other suggestions?||||reg||1
Z||000007||PAUL88GT||12-18-2001||12:50 PM||||Did you try <A HREF="http://www.gmpartsdirect.com?" TARGET=_blank>www.gmpartsdirect.com?</A>  They should be able to beat the Fiero Store prices.||||reg||1
Z||000008||terryk||12-18-2001||01:51 PM||||I bought a set from GM about 4 months ago and paid $100 each. They had to search the dealers for them so that implies they are no longer available. MIne came out of St. Louis I think.<P>At the same time, if an extension was made, many of the rack and pinion inner tie rods would work. The Fiero's rod are just very long rack inner tie rods with a pin welds on to keep them from rotation when you tighten the bolt.<P>Finally, They don't go bad all that often. The outers are more likely to go first. <p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||jelly2m8||12-18-2001||09:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B>IAt the same time, if an extension was made, many of the rack and pinion inner tie rods would work. The Fiero's rod are just very long rack inner tie rods with a pin welds on to keep them from rotation when you tighten the bolt.<P>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-18-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> I've found a way to use a 91 Lumina car inner tierod to work on the rear of the Fiero much in the method you are thinking about above.<BR> Lemme know if you want details.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Eriamjh||12-18-2001||10:12 PM||||Here;s the poop: 155K with an all new front end.  If I do the rears, I want to replace everything so as not to have to do it anytime soon.<P>So please, share with us the details of making the Lumina rods work with a Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000011||terryk||12-18-2001||10:23 PM||||Did you move the brackets in? <P>Didn't The Fiero Shop make some lower control arms with the bracket and then used short tie rods? It would certainly help the bump steer.||||reg||1
